      "first_s": 0.7201542279999558,
      "text": "```python\ndef median(numbers: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the median of a nonempty list of numbers.\n    \n    For an odd-length list, the median is the middle element.\n    For an even-length list, the median is the average of the two middle elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"List must be nonempty\")\n    \n    sorted_nums = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_nums)\n    mid = n // 2\n    \n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return float(sorted_nums[mid])\n    else:\n        return (sorted_nums[mid - 1] + sorted_nums[mid]) / 2.0\n\n\n# Assert tests\nassert median([1, 3, 5]) == 3.0\nassert median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5\n```\n\nThe function handles even-length lists by first sorting the input and computing the middle index `mid = n // 2`. Since the length is even, there is no single middle element. Instead, the two central elements reside at indices `mid - 1` and `mid`. The function retrieves both values, sums them, and divides by 2.0 to compute their arithmetic mean. This average is returned as the median, ensuring the result accurately represents the central tendency of the dataset.<|im_end|>",
